  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Alternate Cuckoo Leafcutter Bee (Coelioxys alternatus) is a small, dark bee species belonging to the cuckoo bee group, which are parasitic breeders that lay eggs in the nests of other bee species. Despite its name referencing plants, this is actually a bee species, not a plant, and it's found throughout New England where it depends on leafcutter bees as hosts.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Distinctive Abdominal Pattern",
      "description": "Features alternating bands of dark and lighter-colored setae (hairs) on the abdomen, creating a striped appearance that gives the species its 'alternatus' name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Body Size",
      "description": "Typically 6-8mm in length, making it noticeably smaller than many other bee species in the region",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pointed Abdomen",
      "description": "Has a characteristic conical or pointed abdomen tip, typical of Coelioxys cuckoo bees, which aids in laying eggs in host nests",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species inhabits gardens, meadows, and woodland edges throughout New England where leafcutter bees (Megachile species) are present, as these host bees are essential for its reproductive success.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, the Alternate Cuckoo Leafcutter Bee is a nest parasite that sneaks into the carefully prepared leaf-lined brood cells of leafcutter bees and lays its own eggs, allowing the host bee to unknowingly provision food for the cuckoo bee's larva.",
